<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Deliberately empty.

  This file was copied wholesale from PrayerWidgetExtension when phase 0 split
  the two extensions apart, and it arrived carrying an App Group it has never
  needed. A Live Activity is not a widget that reads shared storage: ActivityKit
  hands the extension its ActivityAttributes and ContentState, the app pushes
  every update through that channel, and nothing in this target opens
  UserDefaults, a group container, or a file. Grep it: PrayerLiveActivityWidget
  and PrayerLiveActivityAttributes import ActivityKit, SwiftUI, WidgetKit,
  UIKit and Foundation, and touch no storage at all.

  Claiming group.com.prayerapp anyway was not free. An entitlement has to be
  authorised by a provisioning profile, the profile is generated from the App
  ID's capabilities, and automatic signing on Xcode Cloud will not create or
  reconfigure an App ID for you. So the export step failed on every build from
  phase 0 onward, first with

    Automatic signing cannot register bundle identifier
    "com.hassan.prayerapp.MihrabLiveActivity"

  and then, once the App ID was registered by hand, with "cannot update" it,
  because the App Group still had to be attached to it and that is portal-only.
  Local archives never noticed, because they export against a profile that
  already exists on the machine. The 2.9.1 cut is what surfaced it.

  Asking for nothing is both the honest description of this target and the
  thing that makes it sign itself: no capabilities to reconcile, so automatic
  signing has an App ID it can use as-is. If a future Live Activity genuinely
  needs shared storage, add the group back here AND enable App Groups on the
  App ID in the developer portal, in that order, and expect the first cloud
  build after the change to be the one that tells you if you forgot.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
</dict>
</plist>
